<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Scalovians</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1277 CE (approx.) · capital Ragnit</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Baltic people of the lower Neman river, between the Prussians and Lithuanians, conquered by the Teutonic Order in the 1270s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Scalovians, or Skalvians, lived on both banks of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> in what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>. They were related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/curonians">Curonians</a>, and their territory lay on the river route linking the Baltic coast with inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in farming and fishing communities defended by hillforts, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragnit">Ragnit</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a>. Their position made them a buffer between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and they traded and intermarried with both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> campaigned against them from the 1270s, and by 1277 Scalovia was subdued. The Order built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragnit">Ragnit</a> castle in 1289 on the site of a Scalovian fort. Many Scalovians fled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and the region later became part of the area known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> Minor.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/scalovians.jpg" alt="Scalovians" width="320" height="353"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baltic_Tribes_c_1200.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1277 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ragnit</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Baltic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Skalvian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>, <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Skalvians, Scalovia, Skalva</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1038&amp;focus=scalovians" aria-label="Scalovians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5605 203 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Scalovians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5451 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5925 459h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5925 459h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1038&amp;focus=scalovians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

